==English==

===Etymology===
From French ''[[funambule]]'' or its source, Latin ''[[funambulus]]'', from ''[[funis]]'' ‘rope’ + ''[[ambulare]]'' ‘walk’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fjuːˈnæmbjʊlɪst/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[tightrope walker]] or a similar [[performer]] on a [[slack]] rope.
#* '''2012''' June 14, David Hakim, "[http://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/nyregion/nik-wallenda-is-set-to-cross-niagara-falls-friday.html?_r=0 After Century-Long Wait, Stage Is Set for Man Daring to Cross the Falls]," ''New York Times'' <small>(retrieved 1 August 2013)</small>:
#*: A female '''funambulist''', Maria Spelterini, on various occasions tightrope-walked across the Niagara Gorge with peach baskets on her feet, blindfolded, or manacled.

